    id: "2c219fb1151fd1f181df",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Trump says US will not strike Iran before midterm elections",
    summary: "Oil prices drop as Trump dismisses speculation of pre-midterm military action against Iran.",
    preview: "Oil prices drop as Trump dismisses speculation of pre-midterm military action against Iran. United States President Donald Trump has said the US will not attack Iran before the November 3 midterm elections. The US president said late on Thursday: “we are having productive discussions with the Islamic Republic of Iran,” in a post on Truth Social. “We will not be attacking Iran at any time prior to the midterm elections to be held in the United States on November 3rd.” Recommended Stories list of 3 items - list 1 of 3At least 81 US aircraft worth up to $3.",
    body: "Oil prices drop as Trump dismisses speculation of pre-midterm military action against Iran.\n\nUnited States President Donald Trump has said the US will not attack Iran before the November 3 midterm elections.\n\nThe US president said late on Thursday: “we are having productive discussions with the Islamic Republic of Iran,” in a post on Truth Social. “We will not be attacking Iran at any time prior to the midterm elections to be held in the United States on November 3rd.”\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3At least 81 US aircraft worth up to $3.3bn lost or damaged in Iran war\n\n- list 2 of 3Putin pledges Russia’s support to end US-led war on Iran\n\n- list 3 of 3Key takeaways from US Senate debate in Michigan between El-Sayed, Rogers\n\nOil prices fell early on Friday as Middle East supply concerns eased somewhat after tensions in the energy-rich Gulf region had mounted in recent weeks.\n\nThe possibility of renewed fighting had appeared to drive up oil prices in trading on Thursday. Some analysts believe Trump’s comments were an attempt to try and bring prices back down.\n\nIran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Tehran is reviewing the US response to its seven-day plan to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, and that Iran will respond within the “next few days”.\n\nHe added that negotiations are continuing through mediators, Iran’s Tasnim news agency reported.\n\nHormuz explosions\n\nIranian media reported “massive explosions” in the southern Strait of Hormuz on Friday, with Fars citing unnamed military sources as saying tankers using “unauthorised” routes may have struck sea mines.\n\nOn Tuesday, US Vice President JD Vance said Iran must make a “meaningful” reduction in its uranium enrichment capacity as part of an agreement to end the war. Experts say this demand may signal a softening of Washington’s earlier insistence that Tehran abandon enrichment altogether.\n\nOn Monday, Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian said negotiations with Washington were “futile”, accusing the US of using negotiations to attack Iran.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin told Pezeshkian that Russia will “do everything” to help end the US-led war on Iran, Russian news agencies reported on Thursday.",
